{"availability":"available","full":"MarcoKuehne/marcokuehne.github.io","name":"marcokuehne.github.io","description":"Becoming Fluent in Data","category":"Other","language":"JavaScript","stars":4,"forks":4,"created":"2021-04-30T15:06:09Z","updated":"2026-09-09T10:40:34Z","license":null,"branch":"main","homepage":"https://marcokuehne.github.io/","topics":["data","data-science","oer","regression"],"demo":"https://marcokuehne.github.io/","color":"#302e55","ink":"#dbd2ff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-09T11:01:13.621Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-09T11:01:13.621Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-09T11:01:13.621Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-09T11:01:13.621Z","stars":4,"forks":4}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["An open textbook on empirical research with R: how data comes to exist, what shape it arrives in, and what can honestly be concluded from it.","Most statistics courses start with the test and work backwards. This book starts with the data: where it came from, who decided what to measure, what shape it arrived in, and what it quietly leaves out. Methods appear when a question needs them, and each one is worked through on real data — a stork census, a German household panel, seven cohorts of students, eighty-seven dog breeds — so that every number on the page can be recomputed from the repository.","It is written for two audiences. Students get a route from no prior knowledge to reading, producing and criticising empirical work, with interactive exercises in every chapter. Instructors get an open educational resource with a modular structure: chapters and sections stand alone well enough to be reordered, excerpted, or dropped into an existing syllabus."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/MarcoKuehne/marcokuehne.github.io/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"77e96f0d9832d675be2a41a28e4da54d45caa89ea7f07b1cf833bfda2684c397","extractedAt":"2026-10-09T11:01:13.906Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-09T11:01:13.906Z","projectInsights":{"